WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
So far, we have used modules that already exist.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.527
Now we are going to think about creating our own module.

00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:11.822
A module can simply be a Python file that you write yourself.

00:00:11.822 --> 00:00:17.347
For example, imagine a project with two files: main.py and calculations.py.

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:24.082
The calculations.py file can store reusable formulas, such as calculate_profit or profit_margin.

00:00:24.082 --> 00:00:27.377
The main.py file can run the analysis and use those formulas.

00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:31.987
This structure keeps the main file shorter.

00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:34.987
Instead of placing every helper function directly into main.py,

00:00:34.987 --> 00:00:39.722
we move related helper functions into a separate file.

00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:43.277
This is useful when a script starts to become too long.

00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:48.812
It is also useful when the same function is needed in more than one place.

00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:54.647
A beginner-friendly way to think about this is: if you have a group of related helper functions,

00:00:54.647 --> 00:00:57.332
they may belong in their own module.

00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:03.557
You do not need to build a large software project right away.

00:01:03.557 --> 00:01:09.872
The goal here is to understand the basic structure: one Python file can import useful code from another

00:01:09.872 --> 00:01:10.720
Python file.
